TextureView video on its own CALayer: the decoded CVPixelBuffer as layer contents, no copy and no repaint

A TextureView's decoded video took this path per frame: CocoaPresentVideoFrame
copied the CVPixelBuffer into a new int[] of the frame's size (6.5 MB for a
960x1706 Reel), SurfaceTexture turned it into a Bitmap, onFrameAvailable
invalidated the TextureView and the whole view tree was repainted in Java2D --
26-41% of a core on the Android main thread with a Reel playing.

Now (MACAPK_TEXTUREVIEW_LAYER, default on) the frame goes to a per-surface layer,
the same kind SurfaceView lockCanvas frames use: CocoaPresentVideoFrame asks
SurfaceTexture.macapkNativeVideoKey() which layer the frame belongs to, and the
CVPixelBuffer's IOSurface becomes that layer's contents (retained while on
screen so the decoder pool cannot refill it). TextureView registers in
SurfaceView's key space; the compositor pulls its rect per tick (the visible
part, through setTransform) and, through the new macapkSurfaceCropPacked, the
part of the buffer it shows, applied as contentsRect. TextureView.draw punches
the hole once; a frame invalidates nothing, and onSurfaceTextureUpdated is
still delivered, coalesced, on the main thread. Camera and GL producers keep
the old path.

Measured on Instagram 455, Reels, the video playing in both runs (frames differ
between captures): process CPU 38-135% -> 15-18% of a core, RSS 1064 -> 849 MB;
the Java threads 40% -> 14% (the rest JIT warm-up). A feed video half off the
screen shows its top half. Boot 10/10.

